Compute exact eigenvalues when Lanczos terminates early

Before each step, `linesearch()` estimates how far it can move while staying
inside the cone. It does this with Lanczos iteration, but only computes
eigenvalue estimates once it has 5 or more Lanczos vectors. If Lanczos stops
before then, because the Krylov subspace became invariant (always the case when
`X` has fewer than 5 rows), the step is never limited. The barrier parameter
then collapses early, and the iterate lands on the edge of the cone. From there
the Newton steps stop satisfying the constraints.

Instead, when Lanczos stops early, compute the eigenvalues anyway (they are
exact at that point) and use them to limit the step.

Reasoning for not upstreaming this patch: upstream is dead.

--- lib/linesearch.c	2026-09-28 11:26:02
+++ lib/linesearch.c	2026-09-28 11:26:02
@@ -256,6 +256,19 @@
 	{
 	  if (printlevel >= 3)
 	    printf("Small beta[j]\n");
+	  /*
+	   * The Krylov subspace is invariant, so the eigenvalues of the
+	   * current tridiagonal matrix are exact.  Use them, since otherwise
+	   * for small n (Lanczos never reaching j>=5) maxeig is never set.
+	   */
+	  for (i=1; i<=j-1; i++)
+	    lbeta2[i]=lbeta[i]*lbeta[i];
+	  for (i=1; i<=j; i++)
+	    evalues[i]=lalpha[i];
+	  qreig(j,evalues,lbeta2);
+	  for (i=1; i<=j; i++)
+	    if (evalues[i] > maxeig)
+	      maxeig=evalues[i];
 	  j=j-1;
 	  jj=j;
 	  goto DONEEARLY;
